<h2 id="choose-2250">Choose the DCM 2250 TR If</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Your work is on AC systems: panels, motors, feeders, lighting and distribution boards.</li>
  <li>You want the extra 6 A range for small AC loads, with finer resolution than a 60 A range gives.</li>
  <li>You want True-RMS accuracy on non-sinusoidal loads, plus NCV and Low-Z ACV for ghost-voltage checks, in a straightforward AC instrument.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="choose-5410">Choose the DCM 5410 TR If</h2>

<ul>
  <li>You work with <strong>DC current</strong>: solar strings, battery banks, UPS and charger circuits, DC control panels.</li>
  <li>You want capacitance, frequency and temperature in the same instrument, for example checking a motor-run capacitor or a hot connection.</li>
  <li>You want one clamp that covers both AC and DC so a team does not carry two.</li>
</ul>

<Callout type="info" title="The simple rule">
  If you will never clamp a DC cable, the DCM 2250 TR covers AC work with a simpler feature set. If there is any chance you will, buy the DCM 5410 TR now. An AC-only clamp reads zero on DC and cannot be upgraded later.
</Callout>

<h2 id="shared">What They Share</h2>

<p>
  Both measure True-RMS AC current, which matters on loads fed by drives and switching supplies where an average-responding meter would read wrong (see <a href={u('/blog/true-rms-vs-average-rms/')}>True-RMS vs average-responding</a>). Both include Low-Z ACV for separating ghost voltage from real supply (see <a href={u('/blog/ghost-voltage-low-z-ncv-explained/')}>ghost voltage explained</a>), both have data hold, a backlight and a torch (the DCM 2250 TR adds MIN/MAX, and the DCM 5410 TR adds relative-value and inrush-current modes), and both come with test leads, a carrying pouch and a manual. The DCM 5410 TR also comes with a K-type temperature probe.
</p>

